      "id": 1,
      "text": "Red Lake Falls sits where the Red Lake River meets the Clearwater River, a key spot for early fur traders.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Lake_Falls%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1796 a North West Company fur post appeared here, making it one of Minnesota’s oldest European‑occupied sites.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Lake_Falls%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town grew around a flour mill built in 1878 by Earnest Buse and Otto Kankel at the river confluence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Lake_Falls%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Red Lake Falls became the county seat when Red Lake County split from Polk County in 1896, a status it keeps today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Lake_Falls%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Two separate Minnesota communities share the name Red Lake Falls—one in Red Lake County and another in Polk County.",
